# What may be copied into the build context, and — more to the point — what may not.
#
# A .dockerignore is a secrets boundary, not a size optimisation. The build stage does
# `COPY ["ING eBay AutoLister/", ...]`, so anything the context carries is written into an image
# layer; a file deleted in a later layer is still sitting in the one before it, readable by
# anybody who pulls the image. The publish itself already drops credentials.json (see the Content
# Remove rules in the csproj) — that is the second lock on the same door, not this one's excuse.
#
# So: exclude everything, allow the one project back in, and then take the dangerous things out
# again explicitly. The re-exclusions below come after the allow on purpose — last match wins, so
# nothing under the project directory can arrive by way of the "!" line above it.

*

!ING eBay AutoLister/


# ── Secrets. Never in the context, never in a layer, never in the repository ─────────────────
# credentials.json is the live secrets of whichever machine is building: the eBay Client Secret,
# the seller's OAuth refresh token, the Anthropic key, the Stripe secret and webhook secret. It
# sits in the project folder and in dist/ after an installer build. The hosted build never reads
# it — every user's credentials are an encrypted row in the database — so there is nothing to
# lose by refusing it and a whole set of keys to lose by shipping it.
**/credentials.json
**/secrets.json
**/*.env
**/.env*
web-credentials.json

# Saved browser sessions: real login cookies for the seller's own eBay and Facebook accounts.
# A copy of one of these is the account.
**/facebook-session.json*
**/terapeak-session.json*
**/terapeak-profile/

# ── Databases ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every *.db, as asked, plus the two sidecar files SQLite writes beside one — a -wal holds
# committed transactions that are not in the .db yet, so shipping it is shipping the data.
# A hosted deployment's database is a volume (HOSTING.md), never a thing baked into the image:
# an image with a database in it starts every deployment holding whoever's data was on the
# build machine, and overwrites the real one at the mount point.
**/*.db
**/*.db-wal
**/*.db-shm
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite3

# ── Build output and runtime data ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# obj/ especially: a stale project.assets.json from a Windows desktop restore is exactly the file
# the Dockerfile's own restore is trying to produce, and letting one in makes the build fail in a
# way that reads as a dependency problem. dist/ is the desktop installer staging folder, which is
# how a full unredacted credentials.json used to reach a publish.
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/dist/
**/App_Data/
**/photos/
**/generated-photos/
**/wwwroot/url_*.png
**/wwwroot/rembg_*.png
**/*.log

# ── Noise ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
**/.git/
**/.vs/
**/.vscode/
**/*.user
**/*.suo
**/.DS_Store
**/Thumbs.db
